<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamluk Sultanate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mamluk Sultanate was a state ruled by mamluk soldier elites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1250 to 1517. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, halting their advance. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1516 to 1517.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate.jpg" alt="Mamluk Sultanate" width="320" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamluk_Sultanate_of_Cairo_1317_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A mamluk was a freed slave soldier, trained in martial arts, court etiquette and Islamic sciences and expected to stay loyal to his former master. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> had served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> since at least the 9th century, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, they were the backbone of Egypt's army. Most were Kipchak Turks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 1249 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> captured Damietta in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a>, and the Ayyubid sultan al-Salih died. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> promoted his own entourage at the expense of his father's mamluks, and on 2 May 1250 disgruntled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of that corps assassinated him at Fariskur, which established the sultanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Sultan Qutuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1260, halting their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nam-tien">southward expansion</a>, and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>' Syrian principalities. Baybars re-established an Abbasid caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, though with a ceremonial role. By the end of the 13th century Baybars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> and al-Ashraf Khalil had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and expanded into Makuria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Historians call the period to 1382 Bahri and that from 1382 to 1517 Burji or Circassian. Stability and prosperity marked the third reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> (1310–1341).</p><p>Foreign invasions, tribal rebellions and natural disasters then eroded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> authority, and the state fell into financial distress. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a> monopolised trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to submit in 1426. The last effective sultan, Qansuh al-Ghuri, was killed in battle against the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> in 1516, and Selim conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1517, ending Mamluk rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The sultan was the supreme authority and delegated power to provincial governors, mamluk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> called nuwwab al-saltana. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> left the Ayyubid administrative, legal and economic systems largely unchanged, but their state was unitary, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> holding absolute paramountcy over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Entry into the military hierarchy was essentially restricted to mamluks, and revenue came largely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>', a right to collect revenue from a fixed territory, granted to emirs to provision their soldiers.</p><p>The economy was mainly agrarian, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> also stood at the centre of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Cairo reached the peak of its size and wealth before the modern period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> enamelled glass, inlaid metalwork and illuminated Qur'an manuscripts were prized around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Mamluk architecture still makes up much of historic Cairo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mamluk Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even after the Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258, the shadow caliphate maintained by the <b>Mamluks</b> in Cairo (1261–1517) gave legitimacy to Muslim rulers across North Africa and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty's greatest unintended legacy was the <b>Mamluk Sultanate</b> that replaced it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the accession of Barquq in 1382 until 1517, most sultans of <b>Mamluk Egypt</b> and Syria were Circassians who had been brought to Cairo as slave soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Enslaved Kipchak youths were sold to <b>Mamluk Egypt</b>, whose rulers became allies of the khans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate also benefited from diplomatic and commercial contacts with European powers seeking allies against the <b>Mamluks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Divided by baronial quarrels and by the War of Saint Sabas (1256–1258) between Venice and Genoa, the kingdom faced the <b>Mamluk Sultanate</b>, which defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
